<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gansu Corridor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hexi Corridor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> Corridor, also called the Hexi Corridor, is a narrow strip of land in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between mountains and desert, linking China with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>'s seizure of it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> deprived them of valuable grazing lands and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor.jpg" alt="Gansu Corridor" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Han Lei · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linze,_Zhangye,_Gansu,_China_-_panoramio_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kushans were one of five branches of the Yuezhi, a people driven from the <b>Gansu corridor</b> by the Xiongnu in the 2nd century BCE, who settled in Bactria, north of the Hindu Kush, by about 130 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Tiefu-led Helian Xia in 426 and took its capital Tongwancheng in 427; the Northern Liang in the <b>Hexi Corridor</b> submitted as a vassal, the Northern Yan fell in 436, and in 439 Taiwu conquered the Northern Liang outright, uniting the north and closing the Sixteen Kingdoms period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Xiongnu defeated the Yuezhi in the <b>Gansu corridor</b> around 176 BCE, the Yuezhi moved west into the Ili valley and Zhetysu and drove out the Saka who lived there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Conquest added wealth: tribute from the Tuyuhun, Nanzhao and the oasis cities of the Tarim Basin, and from the 760s the farmland and trade of the <b>Hexi Corridor</b> and Dunhuang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heart was the <b>Hexi Corridor</b>, a stretch of the Silk Road and the chief route between northern China and Central Asia; agriculture, pastoralism and trade with Central Asia were the economy's foundations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…great expeditions of the generals Wei Qing and Huo Qubing between 129 and 119 BCE, which seized the <b>Hexi Corridor</b> in 121 BCE and culminated in the Battle of Mobei north of the Gobi in 119 BCE, were the most successful Han counter-offensives and marked the beginning of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
